(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-10"] = {"10.1":{"s":"Halogen derivatives form when halogen atoms replace hydrogen in a hydrocarbon. By skeleton they are haloalkanes, haloalkenes, haloalkynes and haloarenes; by number of halogens, mono, di, tri or poly. Monohalogen compounds are further split by the carbon holding the halogen: alkyl halides (sp³, primary, secondary or tertiary), allylic and benzylic halides (sp³ next to a double bond or ring), and vinylic and aryl halides (halogen on an sp² carbon).","w":["Benzyl chloride (C₆H₅CH₂Cl) is not an aryl halide: its chlorine is on an sp³ carbon."]},"10.2":{"s":"Common names give the alkyl group followed by 'halide' (methyl iodide, tert-butyl chloride). In IUPAC names the halogen is a prefix (fluoro, chloro, bromo, iodo) on the parent hydrocarbon: haloalkanes and haloarenes. For dihalogen derivatives of benzene, common names use ortho, meta and para; IUPAC names use the locants 1,2, 1,3 and 1,4."},"10.3":{"s":"Alkyl halides are best made from alcohols, using HX (Lucas reagent, HCl with ZnCl₂, for less reactive alcohols), phosphorus halides or thionyl chloride. They also come from alkanes and alkenes (adding HX, or allylic substitution at high temperature). Finkelstein (NaI in acetone) and Swarts (metal fluorides) reactions exchange halogens. Aryl chlorides and bromides come from halogenating benzene with a Lewis acid, or from diazonium salts (Sandmeyer reaction).","w":["Reactivity of alcohols with HX: 3° > 2° > 1°.","Thionyl chloride is preferred because its by-products SO₂ and HCl are gases and escape."]},"10.4":{"s":"The carbon-halogen bond is polar, so alkyl halides have dipole-dipole forces and boil higher than the alkanes they come from. Boiling point rises with molecular size: from fluoride to iodide, and with more halogen atoms. Branching lowers it. They are only slightly soluble in water, since they cannot form hydrogen bonds with it, but dissolve in organic solvents.","w":["C–X bond strength falls from C–F to C–I as the halogen gets bigger; so reactivity rises R–F < R–Cl < R–Br < R–I."]},"10.5":{"s":"A carbon atom joined to four different groups is chiral, and a molecule with one such atom cannot be superimposed on its mirror image. The two mirror-image forms, enantiomers, have the same physical properties except that they rotate plane polarised light equally in opposite directions (dextro and laevo). An equal mixture (racemic) shows no rotation. Configurations are drawn as Fischer projections or wedge formulas.","w":["2-chlorobutane is chiral; 2-chloropropane is not (C-2 carries two CH₃ groups)."]},"10.6":{"s":"The carbon in C–X is electrophilic, so nucleophiles replace the halogen. SN2 happens in one step with backside attack, follows second order kinetics and inverts the configuration (Walden inversion); it is fastest for primary halides. SN1 goes through a carbocation, follows first order kinetics, gives a racemic product and is fastest for tertiary halides. With alcoholic KOH they eliminate HX to give alkenes (Saytzeff rule). Aryl halides resist nucleophilic substitution but undergo electrophilic substitution at ortho and para positions.","w":["SN2: 1° > 2° > 3°. SN1: 3° > 2° > 1°.","Aqueous KOH mainly substitutes (alcohol); alcoholic KOH mainly eliminates (alkene)."]},"10.7":{"s":"Several polyhalogen compounds are useful but harmful. Dichloromethane is a solvent and aerosol propellant. Chloroform is a solvent; in light and air it forms poisonous phosgene, so it is stored in dark bottles. Carbon tetrachloride is a solvent but damages the liver. Iodoform was used as an antiseptic. Freons (chlorofluorocarbons) were used in refrigerators and aerosols; they damage the ozone layer.","w":["Chloroform is stored in dark, filled bottles (with a little ethanol) to stop phosgene forming."]}};
